<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mixed-member-proportional-representation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mixed-member proportional representation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as MMP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mixed-member proportional representation, or MMP, is an electoral system in which voters elect constituency members and also vote for parties, with list seats allocated so that each party's share of parliament matches its share of the party vote. Developed in post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, it was adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> after referendums in 1992 and 1993 and first used in 1996, producing coalition or minority governments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mixed-member-proportional-representation.jpg" alt="Mixed-member proportional representation" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rankedchoicevoter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electoral_systems_mmp.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
